{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794651441019,"sessionID":"ses_e26711045ffehqquTcyhnlkCU0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call-691d7efe-b2cb-46bf-88e6-3bb212074d3c","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/record && python3 record.py --help"},"output":"usage: record.py [-h] [--ledger LEDGER]\n                 {ingest,verify,head,range,check-anchors,monitor} ...\n\nexagium record: an append-only, hash-chained ledger of plans and verdicts, with outside anchors.\n\nLayout (under --ledger, default ../ledger):\n  entries/000001.json ...   one entry per file; CI (.github/workflows/record.yml) is the only writer\n  anchors/NNNNNN.sigstore.json   Sigstore attestation by the writer of the range of entries it added, ending at NNNNNN\n  anchors/NNNNNN.ots             OpenTimestamps proof of entry NNNNNN's bytes\n  inbox/*.json                   proposals {\"kind\": ..., \"body\": ...}; CI moves them into entries/\n\nAn entry's identity is the sha256 of its stored bytes, which must be exactly its RFC 8785 (JCS) form\n(no trailing newline). The rules that make Python's json.dumps output equal JCS are enforced: ASCII\nkeys, no floats, integers within +-(2**53-1), no lone surrogates.\n\nCommands: ingest | verify | head | range | check-anchors | monitor.\nExit codes: 0 ok; 1 the ledger, an anchor or the monitor's finding is wrong; 2 can't check (missing\nlibrary or network); 3 unexpected failure. A failure is never reported as a verdict.\n\npositional arguments:\n  {ingest,verify,head,range,check-anchors,monitor}\n    range               print the writer's predicate for entries FROM..TO\n                        (used by CI)\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  --ledger LEDGER\n","metadata":{"output":"usage: record.py [-h] [--ledger LEDGER]\n                 {ingest,verify,head,range,check-anchors,monitor} ...\n\nexagium record: an append-only, hash-chained ledger of plans and verdicts, with outside anchors.\n\nLayout (under --ledger, default ../ledger):\n  entries/000001.json ...   one entry per file; CI (.github/workflows/record.yml) is the only writer\n  anchors/NNNNNN.sigstore.json   Sigstore attestation by the writer of the range of entries it added, ending at NNNNNN\n  anchors/NNNNNN.ots             OpenTimestamps proof of entry NNNNNN's bytes\n  inbox/*.json                   proposals {\"kind\": ..., \"body\": ...}; CI moves them into entries/\n\nAn entry's identity is the sha256 of its stored bytes, which must be exactly its RFC 8785 (JCS) form\n(no trailing newline). The rules that make Python's json.dumps output equal JCS are enforced: ASCII\nkeys, no floats, integers within +-(2**53-1), no lone surrogates.\n\nCommands: ingest | verify | head | range | check-anchors | monitor.\nExit codes: 0 ok; 1 the ledger, an anchor or the monitor's finding is wrong; 2 can't check (missing\nlibrary or network); 3 unexpected failure. A failure is never reported as a verdict.\n\npositional arguments:\n  {ingest,verify,head,range,check-anchors,monitor}\n    range               print the writer's predicate for entries FROM..TO\n                        (used by CI)\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  --ledger LEDGER\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /workspace/record && python3 record.py --help","time":{"start":1794651440954,"end":1794651441007}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"All existing tests pass. Now let me verify the new functionality works by creating a quick test. Let me test the `--max-pending-hours` option manually.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1d98fc736001T0fBrrBIQ5Rb4T","sessionID":"ses_e26711045ffehqquTcyhnlkCU0","messageID":"msg_1d98fc051001wqVAzL5qrz6601"}}
